window.products.push({"id":769841,"name":"“The Flowers of the Month” Art Print by Susan Loy Literary Calligraphy","category":"homeandappliances","retail price":39.0,"sale price":null,"image":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/81T9RylxFXL._AC_SL1000_.jpg","description":"Susan Loy painted two flowers for each month, based on Victorian-era North American and Old English flower calendars. She presents The Flowers of the Month in a vibrant color wheel that reflects the change of seasons. The flowers: January: Carnation or Snowdrop, February: Violet or Primrose, March: Daffodil or Violet, April: Sweet Pea or Daisy, May: Lily of the Valley or Hawthorn, June: Rose or Honeysuckle, July: Larkspur or Water Lily, August: Gladiola or Poppy, September: Aster or Morning Glory, October: Calendula or Hop, November: Chrysanthemum, December: Paperwhite Narcissus or Holly. Susan Loy also hand-lettered a poem for each of the four seasons: Spring “Between the acres of the rye, with a hey, and a ho, and a hey nonino, … how that a life was but a flower.” William Shakespeare, As You Like It; Summer “A something in a summer’s day as slow her flambeau burn away, … And still within a summer’s night a something so transporting bright, I clap my hands to see.” Emily Dickinson; Autumn “When the frost is on the punkin and the fodder’s in the shock, … when the heat of summer’s over and the coolin’ fall is here.” James Whitcomb Riley; Winter “That grand old poem called Winter is round again… So sweet and wholesome is the winter, so simple…” Henry David Thoreau, Journal, Sunday, December 7, 1856. The oldest known flower calendars were created by the Chinese and present flowers that bloom in Asia. Western calendars depicting The Flowers of the Month and the Language of Flowers began to appear in the 18th century and were formalized in England and North America during the 19th century. Signed print produced from Susan Loy’s original watercolor includes bio and information sheet inserted with acid-free mat board into a clear plastic envelope. Print Image Size: 9-1/2” x 9-1/2 Print Paper Size: 11” x 11-1/2”","images":["https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/81T9RylxFXL._AC_SL1000_.jpg","https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/61zNIJh6ZCL._SL1500_.jpg","https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/61TWUez1VxL._AC_SL1500_.jpg","https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/51TIpnkrEpL._AC_SL1500_.jpg","https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/51I6jTu8yCL._AC_SL1300_.jpg","https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/31o9GyU6jnL._AC_.jpg"],"specifications":{"brand":"Literary Calligraphy","source_asin":"B078YF1Q2M","category_path":"Home & Kitchen"},"sku":"BM-769841","productType":"Home & Appliances","inventory":0,"age_group":"Adult","gender":"Unisex","source_url":"https://www.amazon.com/dp/B078YF1Q2M","source_catalog":"Amazon Reviews 2023 product metadata","source_file":"meta_Home_and_Kitchen_2023.jsonl","source_asin":"B078YF1Q2M","price_pending":true,"merchant_ready":false});
